stage3_gold.comments:
[walterddr]: qq: not sure if this solves the problem but can we add `failfast` option to the test runners that runs CUDA test cases?
would this work or should we create a custom test runner that specifically look into each test results and check the CUDA assert failures?

---

[ngimel]: wdym by `failfast` option? If it's aborting after any failure in cuda test, then no, that's not what's requested here. We want to abort only if one of context-invalidating (sticky) errors happened.

---

[walterddr]: oh. yeah I didn't realize Ed meant only sticky errors, thanks for the explanation @ngimel

---

[malfet]: Here is a small  example of sticky errors triggered by on-device assert:

```cpp
#include <stdio.h>
#include <assert.h>

__global__ void assert_kernel() {
  assert (threadIdx.x % 5 != 4);
}

bool test_alloc() {
  void *gpu_vec = nullptr;

  return cudaMalloc(&gpu_vec, 11) == cudaSuccess;
}

bool test_assert() {
  assert_kernel<<<1, 6>>>();
  return cudaDeviceSynchronize() != cudaSuccess;
}

int main(int argc, const char*argv[]) {
  if (argc > 1) {
    printf("test_alloc() %s\n", test_alloc() ? "succeeded" : "failed");
    printf("test_assert() %s\n", test_assert() ? "succeeded" : "failed");
    return 0;
  }
  printf("test_assert() %s\n", test_assert() ? "succeeded" : "failed");
  printf("test_alloc() %s\n", test_alloc() ? "succeeded" : "failed");
  return 0;
}
```
If launched without any argument, it would report that both tests have failed (since test_assert() introduce a non-recoverable error), but if launched with the argument both tests will pass (since `test_assert` is executed at the very end)

---

[ngimel]: Another common sticky error is "Illegal memory access".

[khalilsarwari]: Thank you so much for catching this!!

---

[albanD]: Note that setting `create_graph=False` also fixes the issue, so most likely a special interaction between these two.

---

[albanD]: cc @mfkasim1 I think this is related to https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/43626 but not sure how exactly yet.

---

[soulitzer]: @albanD I think your suspicions are correct. I've created a PR that /should/ fix this. At the very least we know its python related bc the following doesn't leak:

```
#include <c10/cuda/CUDACachingAllocator.h>

torch::autograd::DetectAnomalyGuard detect_anomaly;
for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  auto x = torch::ones({10, 30000}).cuda().requires_grad_();
  auto y = x.exp();
  auto grad = torch::autograd::grad({y}, {x}, {torch::ones_like(y)}, true, true);

  auto stats = c10::cuda::CUDACachingAllocator::getDeviceStats(0);
  std::cout << "allocated: " << stats.allocated_bytes[0].current / (1024 * 1024) << std::endl;
}

```

---

[StalVars]: In my case, I also observed that 'set_detect_anomaly(True)' makes the forward pass much slower.

---

[soulitzer]: That is expected @StalVars 

See the warning in https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/autograd.html#anomaly-detection

The performance hit is because on the creation of every node of the backward graph extra metadata, e.g. current stack trace of the forward execution is stored.

